    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d518">

                <pb facs="0594" n="534" sameAs="frus:frus1895p1/pg534"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Adee to Mr.
                            Runyon</hi>.</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1895-04-22"><hi rendition="#italic">April 22,
                        1895</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 273.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have to inform you that your dispatch,
                    No. 235, of the 2d instant, setting forth the grounds upon which you issued a
                    passport to Miss Dora Schultz, of Chicago, has been received.</p>
                <p>In reply, I have to say that your course in regard to the matter is approved by
                    the Department, as being in accord with the principle established by the
                    Haberacker case (<ref target="frus:frus1891/pg_521">F. R., 1891, p.
                    521</ref>).</p>
                <p>I am, etc.,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Alvey A. Adee</hi>,<lb/><hi
                            rendition="#italic">Acting Secretary</hi>.</signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
